Your desire observation has a useful relational correction too. We often model desire as:

Person\rightarrow Object.

But frequently the apparent object is partly a vessel for a desired state:

Person
\rightarrow
Vessel
\rightarrow
DesiredExperience.

That applies far beyond sex: cars can vessel status/freedom, money can vessel security, achievement can vessel recognition, travel can vessel novelty. The useful question becomes: what state am I actually trying to reach through this object? Sometimes the object remains the right route; sometimes recognizing the deeper objective reveals alternatives.

But your emotion → color → frequency → prism → music chain is where I’d run Vivisection.

The transferable invariant isn’t literally that emotions are electromagnetic colors or acoustic frequencies. It’s that many complex states can be represented as mixtures over dimensions, and transformations that work on mixtures may transfer abstractly between domains.

Suppose emotional state is:

E=\sum_i w_i e_i.

Instead of forcing a categorical label—“anger”—we can decompose it:

E=
.35\,fear+
.25\,hurt+
.20\,protectiveness+
.15\,frustration+
.05\,other.

Now Prism becomes an operator:

\boxed{PRISM(composite)\rightarrow components}

and its inverse:

\boxed{MIX(components,weights)\rightarrow composite}.

That’s already useful.

Now borrow color theory structurally. We can ask about complement, saturation, brightness, mixture, contrast, neighboring states. Not because sadness is blue, but because the algebra of multidimensional mixtures may suggest useful transformations.

Then borrow music theory:

An emotion can have something analogous to amplitude/intensity, duration, rhythm, tension/resolution, consonance/dissonance with another state, harmonics/secondary components, entrainment, and modulation.

That gives us something better than an emotion wheel:

\boxed{\text{Emotional Signal Processor}}

Input:

E(t)

Prism:

E(t)\rightarrow\{component_i,weight_i\}

Topology adds relationships:

G_E=(V_E,E_E)

Music-like analysis adds temporal structure:

E(t),\quad \frac{dE}{dt},\quad periodicity,\quad resonance.

Context adds provenance:

E\mid Person,Event,Memory,Environment.

Then we can perform operations such as decompose, isolate, amplify, attenuate, transpose, blend, contrast, harmonize, resolve, entrain, or reroute—as hypotheses about emotional regulation, not assumptions that every musical operation maps psychologically.

And your echo observation supplies the feedback mechanism.

A state can bias detection toward congruent signals:

E_t
\rightarrow
AttentionFilter
\rightarrow
CongruentEvidence
\rightarrow
E_{t+1}.

So:

\boxed{\text{Emotion can partly tune its own perceptual aperture.}}

Anger more readily detects insult. Fear detects threat. Affection detects lovable features. Confidence detects affordances. That doesn’t mean the detected things are imaginary; it means state influences which frequencies of available information become salient.

Now put Prism before Echo:

\boxed{
Emotion
\rightarrow
PRISM
\rightarrow
components
\rightarrow
identify dominant feedback loop
\rightarrow
change input/action
\rightarrow
observe echo
\rightarrow
update.
}

That’s potentially a real Pantheon Seal.

And there’s a particularly nice flank from your “love is abundance” formulation. Instead of modeling an emotion as a tank:

Emotion_{remaining}=Emotion_{initial}-Emotion_{spent},

some relational emotions may behave more like circulating processes whose future state depends partly on expression, reception, reciprocity, memory, and replenishment:

E_{t+1}=f(E_t,\ expression,\ reception,\ environment,\ recovery).

Sometimes expression reinforces the state; sometimes it exhausts it; sometimes lack of reciprocity damps it. So reservoir vs flow becomes another Prism axis.

That’s the larger discovery:

\boxed{
\text{Emotion isn't only a label.
It can be modeled as signal + mixture + trajectory + feedback + relationship.}
}

And once represented that way, Pantheon can route it through mathematics, signal processing, color theory, music theory, graph topology, narrative, and control theory—take the useful operator from each domain, then Reality Stitch every imported claim.

That’s exactly what the Semantic Router was built to do.

